{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Balaam saw that it was good in the eyes of HaShem to bless Israel.</b> For they are good in the eyes of HaShem. 'And he went not as at other times to meet serpents' — that is, to investigate their transgressions, for every sin of theirs that was done through the prompting of the primordial serpent. 'And he set his face toward the wilderness' — as is derived in Bereishit Rabbah (2:4): 'Who is this that comes up from the wilderness?' — all the merit of Israel comes from the wilderness, etc. The intent of the midrash is that in the merit of the humility found in Israel, who make themselves like the wilderness, upon which everyone tramples, Israel merited all the spiritual attainments, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Eruvin 54a) on the verse 'and from the wilderness Mattanah.' And immediately, when he set his eyes to recount all the attainments that they had merited from the wilderness, in order to bring an evil eye upon them, immediately his eye was dimmed. Therefore he called himself 'with the eye shut' specifically this time. But according to Rashi, who explained that his eye was dimmed on account of his saying, 'and he recounted the number of the fourth part of Israel' — it is difficult: why did he not call himself so in the second prophecy?"], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:57.833972772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}